import{_ as a,d as t,e as i,x as n}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance","xkChars":0,"xkWords":450},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md"}'),o={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-20.md"};function s(r,e,c,l,d,p){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-20" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.20</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-20" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.20 State Capacity, Policy Implementation, and Governance {#section-d-20}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §7.3.7–7.3.8, §10–11, §14, and §15.3.5. Research entries cover 20.01–20.10.</p><p>State formation, fiscal and military mobilization, administrative autonomy, embeddedness, legitimacy of rule, social identification, and public services are distinct processes. Compliance can have several causes. Administrative reach can coexist with gaps in rights. State capacity should be specified by task, level, and organizational resources. One aggregate measure of strength cannot substitute for this analysis.</p><p>A policy chain can distinguish entry of an issue onto the agenda, rulemaking, definition of eligibility, making budgets available, frontline implementation, notification and application, determining and paying benefits, actual use, and correction through appeal. The stages have different denominators and timing. Rates for eligible people, applicants, people approved, and actual recipients must not be mixed. Whether the calculation uses households or individuals can also change the result.</p><p>Frontline discretion can compensate for rules that do not fit or create bias and exclusion. Assess it in light of task conflicts, resources, and oversight. Polycentric governance requires actual authority and coordination. A large number of institutions does not suffice. Anti-corruption and oversight work must check who appoints, who pays, who selects the sample, and who has authority to implement and review. The value of local knowledge does not establish that local elites represent every resident.</p><p>In a teaching example, random inspections improve the completeness of records while remedies for ordinary applicants remain slow. Oversight effects and remedies in individual cases each have their own scope. Temporary aid can improve people’s lives while leaving questions about continuing finance and the right to choose. For an institutional externality, specify which decision accounting excludes it. Harm can be recorded before final legal responsibility is established.</p><p>Specify whom autonomy is relative to and what it concerns. Freedom from party control, discretion under administrative rules, and authority for local bodies or users to change rules are different dimensions. Ties to higher levels can provide resources and remedies or control goals and individual cases. Approval rates, the proportion who have ever received benefits, and continuously available services have different denominators and time conditions. Retain local improvements already observed. Successes among people inside the procedure cannot establish coverage of the entire eligible population.</p><p>Research topics. 20.01 State formation and fiscal-military states (Tilly and others). 20.02 Legitimacy, domination, and identification with the state (Weber and others). 20.03 State capacity, autonomy, and embeddedness (Mann, Evans, Fukuyama, and others). 20.04 Street-level bureaucracy and policy implementation (Lipsky). 20.05 Polycentric governance and the commons (Ostrom). 20.06 Public policy, implementation, and feedback. 20.07 Central–local relations and scale. 20.08 Corruption, capture, and principal–agent relations. 20.09 Comparisons of democratic, authoritarian, bureaucratic, and technocratic governance. 20.10 Governance failure, state fragility, and reconstruction.</p></div>',1)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
